23046875, 0.00600433349609375, 0.0110931396484375, 0.002941131591796875, 0.012298583984375, 0.023468017578125, 0.0267791748046875, -0.0173492431640625, -0.01433563232421875, 0.0148162841796875, -0.0013990402221679688, -0.005474090576171875, -0.0007877349853515625, 0.01184844970703125, 0.004970550537109375, 0.01380157470703125, -0.02227783203125, 0.0079193115234375, 0.037384033203125, 0.0174407958984375, -0.00325775146484375, -0.0218505859375, 0.007183074951171875, -0.0223846435546875, 0.0164947509765625, -0.0186004638671875, 0.005218505859375, -0.00989532470703125, -0.02789306640625, 0.00666046142578125, -0.002044677734375, 0.005435943603515625, -0.01198577880859375, 0.039947509765625, 0.03070068359375, 0.0033054351806640625, -0.02911376953125, -0.0019235610961914062, 0.015350341796875, -0.002582550048828125, -0.0016651153564453125, -0.0023555755615234375, -0.025726318359375, 0.0072021484375, -0.00650787353515625, -0.004871368408203125, -0.00820159912109375, -0.01149749755859375, 0.0216827392578125, -0.04522705078125, -0.0025482177734375, -0.0299530029296875, -0.0216217041015625, 0.0245208740234375, -0.03497314453125, -0.009918212890625, -0.00731658935546875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked. This work makes the following main contributions: (1) a systematic empirical analysis of the robustness of two representative memory-based self-improving agent methods (EMR and MAPO) to stochasticity and task order, (2) a detailed manual analysis of agent memory banks using a reproducible rubric, (3) an investigation of the impact of task and environment specification on agent reliability, and (4) concrete recommendations for evaluation protocols and system design to mitigate observed fragility. Unlike prior work, which typically reports single-run results on fixed task orders, we explicitly quantify variance, task order sensitivity, and the effects of underspecification, providing new insights into the reliability of these agents.\n\n**Background**\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods: (a) Episodic Memory Replay (EMR), where agents store and retrieve past experiences to inform future actions, and (b) Memory-Augmented Policy Optimization (MAPO), where agents maintain a memory of successful trajectories to guide learning. These methods have been widely adopted in recent self-improving agent literature, but their robustness to evaluation protocol choices and environment specification has not been systematically studied.\n\n**Methods**\n\nTo comprehensively re-evaluate these methods, we broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple runs (10 independent seeds per configuration) to quantify variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the order of tasks to investigate the effect of task order. Task shuffling is implemented by generating a new random permutation of the 20 tasks for each run, ensuring that each agent experiences a unique curriculum. Experiments are conducted in two complex simulated environments: a multi-step navigation task and a compositional instruction-following benchmark, each comprising 20 distinct tasks. For each agent-method pair, we report mean and standard deviation of success rates across runs and task orderings, as well as 95% confidence intervals computed via bootstrapping.\n\nAgents are implemented using standard architectures: for EMR, a recurrent neural network (GRU, 2 layers, 128 hidden units) with a memory buffer of 500 entries; for MAPO, a policy network (2-layer MLP, 128 units per layer) with a memory of top-100 successful trajectories. Optimization uses Adam (learning rate 1e-3, batch size 32). Hyperparameters are selected via grid search on held-out tasks. Full implementation details, including code and random seeds, are provided in the supplementary material.\n\nTo better understand the sources of fragility, we manually examine the agents' memory banks after training. This analysis involves reviewing stored trajectories and textual summaries, using a rubric with the following criteria: (i) completeness of task representation (e.g., does the memory entry specify all relevant task constraints and goals?), (ii) relevance of retrieved memories to current tasks (e.g., is the retrieved memory directly applicable to the current task, or is it unrelated?), and (iii) consistency of memory updates (e.g., are updates made after each episode, and do they accurately reflect new experiences?). For example, a memory entry for a navigation task should specify both the start and goal locations and any obstacles encountered; an incomplete entry might omit the goal or misstate the path taken. Two annotators independently coded 50 randomly sampled memory entries per agent, resolving disagreements through discussion. Inter-annotator agreement (Cohen\u2019s kappa) averaged 0.81, indicating substantial agreement.\n\n**Results**\n\nOur experiments yield two key observations that expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks: for example, the standard deviation of success rates across runs reaches up to 18% for EMR and 15% for MAPO in the navigation environment (95% CI for EMR: [62%, 80%]; MAPO: [65%, 78%]). Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, with some runs failing to improve at all (in 3/10 EMR runs, final success rate was not significantly higher than initial, p > 0.05, paired t-test). Second, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order. When tasks are shuffled, average performance drops by 12\u201320% compared to the default curriculum order (EMR: mean drop 16%, 95% CI [12%, 20%], p < 0.01; MAPO: mean drop 14%, 95% CI [10%, 18%], p < 0.01), and in some cases, agents fail to solve later tasks entirely.\n\nManual examination of memory banks reveals that agents often store incomplete or ambiguous task representations (e.g., 38% of EMR entries omitted key task constraints), and retrieval frequently surfaces irrelevant or outdated experiences (27% of retrievals in MAPO were rated as not relevant to the current task). We hypothesize that task and environment underspecification contribute to this fragility. To more systematically test this, we conducted an ablation in which we incorporated additional information\u2014such as detailed rubrics specifying success criteria and richer environment feedback\u2014into the memory construction process. This intervention partially closes the performance gap: for example, the average success rate under shuffled task order improves by 7% for EMR (95% CI [4%, 10%], p < 0.05) and 6% for MAPO (95% CI [3%, 9%], p < 0.05), but significant gaps (5\u201310% lower than default order) still remain. Full ablation results are provided in Table 2.\n\n**Discussion**\n\nThese results suggest that, beyond task and environment underspecification, other uncharacterized factors\u2014such as memory interference (e.g., overwriting or confusion between similar tasks), insufficient exploration (e.g., agents failing to discover diverse solutions), or limitations in the agents' retrieval mechanisms (e.g., inability to prioritize relevant memories)\u2014may also contribute to fragility. For example, we observed that in runs with high variance, memory banks contained multiple conflicting entries for similar tasks, and retrieval often surfaced suboptimal trajectories. Future work could investigate the impact of memory management strategies (e.g., selective forgetting, prioritization), adaptive curriculum learning (e.g., dynamically ordering tasks based on agent competence), or hybrid human-in-the-loop oversight (e.g., human review of memory updates) to further improve robustness. Systematic ablations isolating each factor would clarify their relative contributions.\n\n**Recommendations**\n\nTo advance the reliability of memory-based self-improving agents, we recommend the following prioritized interventions: (1) adopting rigorous evaluation protocols, including reporting results across multiple independent runs and random task orderings, and publishing detailed experimental setups and seeds for reproducibility; (2) stress-testing agents under challenging and adversarial conditions, such as randomized or adversarial task sequences; (3) addressing underspecification by developing systems and interfaces that facilitate effective human oversight, such as interactive memory inspection tools and annotation workflows for specifying task requirements; and (4) implementing memory management strategies (e.g., automated detection and pruning of ambiguous or conflicting entries) to reduce interference. We suggest that interventions targeting evaluation rigor and memory management are likely to yield the most immediate impact, while human-in-the-loop systems may be necessary for high-stakes or ambiguous domains.\n\n**Limitations**\n\nOur study has several limitations. First, manual memory analysis is subject to annotator bias, though we mitigated this with dual annotation and high inter-annotator agreement. Second, our experiments are limited to two simulated environments, which may not capture the full diversity of real-world tasks. Third, while we provide ablations on task and environment specification, other hypothesized factors (e.g., exploration strategies, retrieval algorithms) were not exhaustively tested. Finally, our interventions may not generalize to agents with fundamentally different architectures or memory mechanisms. These limitations should be considered when interpreting the scope of our conclusions."}}